@9000 = ~Durbem's Key
Durbem, a dwarf at the Purple Wyrm Inn, has asked us to recover an intricate golden key that he lost in the sewers. He refuses to reveal what it opens.~
@9001 = ~Durbem's Key
We recovered Durbem's intricately engraved golden key from the sewers and returned it to him at the Purple Wyrm Inn. Our task is complete.~
@9002 = ~Welcome! Welcome, dear travellers. Are you seeking rest from your travels? Or maybe you came to sample one of the finest wines in the whole Baldur's Gate. Oh, hold on. You are… <CHARNAME>. The savior of Nashkel, hero who restored peace to the region. I would recognise that tired face of yours everywhere now that my ears are flooded with tales of your heroic endeavours. Theo the Historian, at your service. Anytime you want to take a short break and listen about the history of the city and areas related to it, hit me up.~
@9003 = ~Are you aspiring to be like Volo?~
@9004 = ~I see my reputation precedes me. So, you are the chronicler of Baldur's Gate history, correct? Have any stories that you're willing to share?~
@9005 = ~Maybe some other time. Time is pressing us at the moment.~
@9006 = ~Hahah, no my friend. Me and Volo are comrades and love exchanging stories between one another but I don't aspire to be anyone beyond my scope, which is very little. All I want is to share the amazing lore of our beloved city with anyone willing to listen. That's all.~
@9007 = ~Fantastic that you ask! I have a whole chain of stories linking to Baldur's Gate city I'd be more than pleased to share with you. What do you want to hear about? The betrayed but never forgotten Eldrith the Betrayer? The disappearance of Balduran - the founder of the city? Or maybe about mysterious ruins that allegedly slumber underneath our feet?~
@9008 = ~Eldrith the Betrayer is a classic. I learned about her back in Candlekeep but please, do go on.~
@9009 = ~Story about disappearance of Balduran on his last voyage sounds intriguing.~
@9010 = ~Tell me about the ruins underneath Baldur's Gate.~
@9011 = ~I want to hear about something else.~
@9012 = ~I think time is pressing us a little. We can continue some other time.~
@9013 = ~Ah, Eldrith. Now there is a tale worth remembering. Though I suspect you know more about her than most travellers already do. Eldrith was once one of the most respected commanders of the Flaming Fist. A capable soldier, a stern leader, and, by all accounts, a woman who believed deeply in order. Yet somewhere along the way, her loyalty to Baldur's Gate began to fracture.~
@9014 = ~When the troubles that threatened the Sword Coast reached their height, Eldrith ultimately turned against the city she had sworn to protect. Her reasons have been debated ever since. Some say she became convinced that the city's rulers were corrupt beyond redemption. Others claim grief and disillusionment drove her to abandon the principles she had once defended. Minority speculated that she herself was betrayed by city rulers but... no one knows for sure.~
@9015 = ~Whatever the truth, the result was the same. She became known as Eldrith the Betrayer. And yet… history has a curious habit of reducing people to a single deed. Betrayer. Traitor. Villain. Those words are convenient, but they rarely tell us why someone reached such a point.~
@9016 = ~Ahhh, Balduran. Now we step away from history and into legend. Or perhaps I should say… into the place where history and legend become almost impossible to separate. Balduran was no ordinary sailor. He was an explorer, adventurer and enormously wealthy merchant who travelled far beyond the shores of the Sword Coast. His name became so closely associated with this city that, eventually, Baldur's Gate itself came to bear it.~
@9017 = ~But then came his final voyage. Balduran sailed westward, seeking new lands and new opportunities. He never returned. And therein lies the mystery. Some accounts claim that he reached a distant island inhabited by a strange people and became involved in a terrible conflict. Others insist that his expedition met disaster somewhere across the sea. There are even sailors who swear that Balduran survived for years after his disappearance, living somewhere beyond the known seas.~
@9018 = ~But where truth ends and tavern tales begin, even the historians cannot say. What we do know is that Balduran never came home. His fortune was left behind, his expedition vanished, and his name became immortal. A rather fitting fate for a man who spent his life chasing the unknown, wouldn't you say? After all, Baldur's Gate remembers him every single day.~
@9019 = ~Most people think the city ends where the streets do. We have a few layers here. The streets, the sewers, network caverns and tunnels and even deeper - the ancient ruins of an old city.~
@9020 = ~The Undercity is particularly fascinating. It has served as a refuge for criminals, a hiding place for cults, and a home to creatures that would rather never see the light of day. Over the centuries, portions of it have been forgotten, sealed away and rediscovered. And that is precisely what makes it so dangerous.~
@9021 = ~Every generation of Baldurians seems to believe that it has discovered the last forgotten passage beneath the city. Boy, were they wrong. There are old stories of ruined temples, abandoned chambers and passages stretching far beyond the places currently known to the city's authorities. Some scholars believe they connect to much older settlements that existed here before Baldur's Gate grew into the city we know today.~
@9022 = ~Before Baldur's Gate was founded by the legendary hero Balduran, the area on the surface was a lawless, muddy harbor town called Gray Harbor, filled with pirates and smugglers. A true utopia for pirates, so to speak. Long before humans settled Gray Harbor, an ancient, unnamed civilization built a subterranean settlement in these caverns. Over centuries, it fell into ruin, becoming a ghost town of stone structures haunted by the undead.~
@9023 = ~Interpretations differ from person to person. Some claim they city is just a remnant of an older pirate cove, others say it was buried due to an earthquake or even that its original inhabitants hid it on purpose to hide their secrets. But not one can tell for sure and the entrances are closed off to the public. I would love to document it in more detail one day.~
@9024 = ~What is on your mind, my friend.~
@9025 = ~Do you have any information on Iron Throne?~
@9026 = ~Do you know of any interesting places within the city worth exploring?~
@9027 = ~Actually, I want to know something else.~
@9028 = ~As a historian I know a lot of things about my beloved birth-city, alas Iron Throne is relatively new and most information is kept from the public. From the outside it appears as massive, highly successful, and honorable merchant guild. In my humble opinion, they wear a clever mask of civic virtue, presenting themselves as the saviors of the Sword Coast, whilst conducting shady businesses and dealings. That's all I can say.~
@9029 = ~What is on your mind?~
@9030 = ~Do you have any information on Iron Throne?~
@9031 = ~Do you know of any interesting places within the city worth exploring?~
@9032 = ~Actually, I want to know something else.~
@9033 = ~Friend, we have in abundance of that. Visit Sorcerous Sundries if you're willing to gear up for tough adventures. Visit Splurging Sturgeon if you want to experience whole new side of pleasure. Visit Hall of Wonders if you're starving for culture appreciation. Visit local sewers if you're already missing dungeons. It's technically a dungeon, just less... refined.~
@9034 = ~Good to see you again, hero.~
@9035 = ~Howdy, traveller. Enjoying your time at Purple Inn so far?~
@9036 = ~Who are you?~
@9037 = ~Inn like any other. Nothing much to do here.~
@9038 = ~We must depart now. Excuse me.~
@9039 = ~My name is Kaeira. I'm a caravan guard for hire. At the moment I work for Randalla Brasshorn. It's lovely to meet you. What brings you to Baldur's Gate?~
@9040 = ~We are searching for well paid jobs.~
@9041 = ~We are performing a task. Forgive me if I can't disclose you all the details.~
@9042 = ~Just exploring.~
@9043 = ~Then I suggest you speak to Randalla. She might have a task for you.~
@9044 = ~Hm, sounds intriguing. But don't worry, I won't pull your tongue. We all have our secrets.~
@9045 = ~As someone who have seen a huge chunk of the world and its inhabitants I can tell there's a little more to you than meets the eye, however I won't press you. We all have our secrets, don't we?~
@9046 = ~Hard to disagree. It's relatively simple to find some good ale, and pork. Entertainment is harder to come by. Drinking saves you from feeling bored out of your life.~
@9047 = ~Wait… ehm… I was hoping you could help me with a small favour, actually. You see, some nights ago I lost a pretty valuable merchandise. A rare bag of spices from Amn. The caravan I was guarding was attacked by bunch of goblins known as Red Fang Marauders. It's a bandit-like group made out of goblins and orcs. They attacked the caravan out of nowhere, caught us off guard. Plenty guards lost their lives.~
@9048 = ~These spices were to be handed to a noble family that paid an exorbitant price for it, and undoubtedly aren't pleased with the outcome. Red Fang Marauders must still have the spices in their collection of loot. If you'd be willing to get out of town with me, I could lead you to where they jumped us and we shall get these spices back together. As a reward you can take whatever loot they collected in their caves. What would you say?~
@9049 = ~I agree. Let's go.~
@9050 = ~Can I have some time to think about it?~
@9051 = ~Conditions of this locale are outrageous. Ale's quality is comparable to tasloi's urine and my stew got served cold. Someone will get sued for this. With me, Hendron! We're leaving this rusty dump!~
@9052 = ~I do not understand why some people lean more toward Elfsong than Purple Wyrm. Alyth is a great person and all, but her tavern unite all kinds of thugs and ruffians whilst this place is peaceful. More friendly.~
@9053 = ~In my youth I dreamed about seeing Forests of Tethyr down south. My grandmother told me so many amazing things about these areas. In fact, I have quite a few elven friends here in the city, and those that hail from Tethyr describe it as magical enclave which seen can never be unseen.~
@9054 = ~Ehm… pardon me, if I don't share your biased view, elf.~
@9055 = ~Do me a favour, good man and focus on cities. Life in Tethyr is boring, monotonous and devoid of thrills and excitement. I left Tethyr to sample more exclusive life, gain fame, wealth and meet ladies of exquisite grace. Cities such as this have it all. Thrill, danger, unpredictability, occasions to get gold. You have no reason to be dreaming about Tethyr, neither in terms of sightseeing. It's just a bunch of trees.~
@9056 = ~Human want eat?~
@9057 = ~Troll as a cook? World has gone crazy.~
@9058 = ~Uncommon to see a troll here.~
@9059 = ~I'll go now.~
@9060 = ~Client not complain. Complain, no food.~
@9061 = ~Humans be open. Cooking well. Be good servant. Be learn since young.~
@9062 = ~Welcome at Purple Wyrm Inn. What service can I provide?~
@9063 = ~Show me what you got.~
@9064 = ~Goodbye.~
@9065 = ~Certainly. Our inn's provisions are being prepared. Come back shortly.~
@9066 = ~Thanks for bringing me my key back, but I'd rather enjoy my ale alone now.~
@9067 = ~I'm glad you had enough common sense to bring me my desired key.~
@9068 = ~What is this key for? Looks intricate.~
@9069 = ~No problem.~
@9070 = ~You better pay as well for all the sewer-crawling. The smell this escapade inflicted on me is unbearable.~
@9071 = ~Still waiting on that golden key, lad. I'll be here with my ale.~
@9072 = ~That, lad, is my business. You've returned it, and that's all you need to know. Now, back to my ale.~
@9073 = ~Aye. You've done me a good turn. Now, if you don't mind, I'd like to enjoy my ale.~
@9074 = ~Hah! Fair enough. Here's a hundred gold for your trouble. Now let me enjoy my ale.~
@9075 = ~You're back. Have you reconsidered my offer?~
@9076 = ~We want to work for you.~
@9077 = ~Nah. We're just checking if you're still sulking.~
@9078 = ~Argh, away with you! Didn't you mother ever teach you to never bother a dwarf in his cups?~
@9079 = ~What if I get you another cup?~
@9080 = ~What if I get you another cup?~
@9081 = ~What if I get you another cup?~
@9082 = ~What if I get you another cup?~
@9083 = ~What if I get you another cup?~
@9084 = ~What if I get you another cup?~
@9085 = ~What if I get you another cup?~
@9086 = ~Farewell.~
@9087 = ~Hah! That's different then! Thanks for the ale, human. What do you want?~
@9088 = ~Do you have any jobs we could undertake?~
@9089 = ~Nothing. Being friendly. Farewell.~
@9090 = ~Hah! That's different then! Thanks for the ale, elf. What do you want?~
@9091 = ~Do you have any jobs we could undertake?~
@9092 = ~Nothing. Being friendly. Farewell.~
@9093 = ~Hah! That's different then! Thanks for the ale, half-elf. What do you want?~
@9094 = ~Do you have any jobs we could undertake?~
@9095 = ~Nothing. Being friendly. Farewell.~
@9096 = ~Hah! That's different then! Thanks for the ale, dwarf. What do you want?~
@9097 = ~Do you have any jobs we could undertake?~
@9098 = ~Nothing. Being friendly. Farewell.~
@9099 = ~Hah! That's different then! Thanks for the ale, halfling. What do you want?~
@9100 = ~Do you have any jobs we could undertake?~
@9101 = ~Nothing. Being friendly. Farewell.~
@9102 = ~Hah! That's different then! Thanks for the ale, gnome. What do you want?~
@9103 = ~Do you have any jobs we could undertake?~
@9104 = ~Nothing. Being friendly. Farewell.~
@9105 = ~Hah! That's different then! Thanks for the ale, half-orc. What do you want?~
@9106 = ~Do you have any jobs we could undertake?~
@9107 = ~Nothing. Being friendly. Farewell.~
@9108 = ~Looking for jobs? Hah! You couldn't have arrived at more unlucky hour. I am in need of assistance and temporarily frozen in place. Me and my fellow - Borador Goldhand were exploring the sewers.~
@9109 = ~I know. Stinky cesspool. Don't even dare to laugh at me. We were getting good coin from exterminating the scum down there. The problem is that I dropped a key down there. Purpose of the key shall remain my business, but I really do need it back. Fast. Would you be willing to look around the sewers and find a golden key?~
@9110 = ~If I'll stumble upon it, I'll bring it to you.~
@9111 = ~Do I look like sewer-dweller? Ask cleaners.~
@9112 = ~That's the spirit, lad. I'll be here, patiently awaiting your return.~
@9113 = ~Disappointing. Perhaps you are much more cowardly than I anticipated. Off with you, and let me enjoy my ale.~
@9114 = ~That's the spirit, lad. I'll be here, patiently awaiting your return.~
@9115 = ~I know. Stinky cesspool. Don't even dare to laugh at me. We were getting good coin from exterminating the scum down there. The problem is that I dropped a key down there. Purpose of the key shall remain my business, but I really do need it back. Fast. Would you be willing to look around the sewers and find a golden key?~
@9116 = ~If I'll stumble upon it, I'll bring it to you.~
@9117 = ~Do I look like sewer-dweller? Ask cleaners.~
@9118 = ~As expected from dwarven miscreants. This one even looks the type.~
